title: for_expdpy
---



```python
for_expdpy(
    data,
    scale=None,
    years=None,
    variables=None,
    labels=None,
    groups=None,
    ref=None,
)
```

``(df, df_def)`` in the format expdpy expects.

## Parameters {.doc-section .doc-section-parameters}

| Name      | Type         | Description                                                 | Default    |
|-----------|--------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|------------|
| data      |              | A dataset id or your own panel with ``geo_id`` and ``year`` | _required_ |
| scale     | str \| None  | As in ``geo.load`` (for a dataset id)                       | `None`     |
| years     | str \| None  | As in ``geo.load`` (for a dataset id)                       | `None`     |
| variables | str \| None  | As in ``geo.load`` (for a dataset id)                       | `None`     |
| labels    | dict \| None | ``{variable: description}`` for your own columns            | `None`     |
| groups    |              | Coarser scales whose unit names to add as columns           | `None`     |

## Notes {.doc-section .doc-section-notes}

These are the parameters of ``for_geometrics``. ``groups`` is handy for expdpy's
by-group views, e.g. ``groups=["department"]`` adds a ``department`` column
(the department's name) for ``ex.explore_trend_plot_by_group``.

## Returns {.doc-section .doc-section-returns}

| Name   | Type   | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |
|--------|--------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|        | dict   | df : the panel with ``geo_id``, ``name``, ``year``, the group names and the variables df_def : the data dictionary (``var_name``, ``var_def``, ``label``, ``type``,     ``role``, ``can_be_na``); pass both to ``ex.set_labels(df, df_def, set_panel=True)`` |

## Example {.doc-section .doc-section-example}

```python
!pip install -q geo4bolivia expdpy
import geo4bolivia as geo
import expdpy as ex

df, df_def = geo.for_expdpy("sdg", variables="index_sdg", groups=["department"])
df = ex.set_labels(df, df_def, set_panel=True)
ex.explore_trend_plot_by_group(df, group_var="department", var="index_sdg").fig
```

